Maxwell's demon has nothing to do with quantum phiscs, but with
_statistical_ physics. 

>The idea is that since molecules are bouncing around against the
>walls of any container, you could have a "doorway" which selectively opened
>when a molecule hit it from the proper side, allowing that molecule
>through, but being impervious from the "wrong" side.  This could be used to
>create a vacuum, manipulate thermal energies, etc.  As I recall, the demon
>comes into this to act as a sort of doorman.

Basically correct. 

Maxwell proposed his demon in a discussion of the second law of
thermodynaimcs, the law that in its most general form says that the
entropyof a closed system can't decrease (see any textbook on thermal
physics for an explanation of what this means). 

Simple, everyday life, instances of the second law are for example
that heat will never spontaneously flow from a colder body to a hotter
one, or that if you open a canister of gas in a room, the gas will
flow out, mixing with the air in the room, but the reverse thing will
never happen spontaneously - the air in the room will not rush into
the canister, leaving the room empty!

Maxwell considered one special case: imagine a gas in a closed
container. Put a gas-tight, thermally insulating wall in the middle of
the container. Heat the gas in one half of the container, and then
open a small hole in the wall. The second law now says that gas should
stream through the hole in such a way that the differences in
temperature and pressure will equalize. 

The statistical mechanics explanation for this is that in the hot gas,
the molecules will move around faster than in the cold gas, which
means that a larger number of the "hot" molecules will pass through
the hole into the cold part of the container than vice versa.

Here, Maxwell's demon enters the picture.

Suppose, says Maxwell, that we put a gatekeeper at the hole, a "demon"
that only lets through fast molecules if they go from the cold side
to the hot side, and only slow molecules if they go from the hot side
to the cold side (there'll always be some deviations from the mean, so
the cold side will contain some fast molecules and the hot side some
slow ones). The result will be that after a while, the hot side
contains even more fast molecules than before, i.e. it is even hotter,
and the cold side contains even more slow molecules, i.e. it is colder
than before.

So Maxwell's demon can cause heat to flow from a colder object to a
hotter one.

In general, Maxwellian demons can be used to do things that seem to
contradict the second law. In Nethack, he could manipulate the air in
the dungeon, creating vacua where you can't breather, or change the
temperature of objects (Maxwell's demon hits -- your potion of foo
freezes!).

